\section{Conclusion}
In this study, we introduce a novel psychological perspective on hallucinations in VLMs, highlighting authority bias as a significant factor alongside phenomena such as sycophancy and logical inconsistencies. We constructed AIpsych, a scalable benchmark designed to analyze and quantify psychological behaviours in VLMs systematically. Our psychoanalysis-inspired taxonomy provides valuable clarity by categorizing and interpreting hallucination behaviours through human psychological analogies, highlighting connections overlooked by traditional bias analyses. Specifically, we attempt to justify authority bias as an explanatory mechanism because it distinctly captures scenarios where models display excessive trust in provided prompts, analogous to human susceptibility to authority figures. Our human survey results further substantiate this analogy by demonstrating similar behavioral patterns between humans and models, underscoring authority bias as a meaningful psychological dimension rather than a mere technical limitation. Thus, AIpsych enriches our understanding of VLM hallucinations beyond conventional bias interpretations.

Furthermore, our extensive evaluation of SOTA VLMs revealed that larger models exhibit increased sycophancy and reduced susceptibility to authority bias, reflecting enhanced spatial interpretability. Interestingly, human surveys aligned closely with model behaviours, underscoring the parallels and distinctions between human cognitive biases and VLM responses. Our findings emphasize the importance of integrating psychological insights into the evaluation and design of trustworthy AI, offering a new perspective for future investigation of the hallucination problem in VLMs.



\section{Limitations}
Despite the insights provided by AIpsych, our study has several limitations that should be acknowledged. First, our benchmark primarily focuses on object colour and position as the manipulated attributes. While these aspects are fundamental for assessing visual understanding, and already pose significant challenges to the tested models.  Future work could extend the benchmark to incorporate more nuanced object properties, such as material and contextual relationships between objects, to provide a more comprehensive evaluation of hallucinations in the VLMs.

Second, AIpsych is built upon the COCO dataset, which, while widely used, may not fully capture the diversity of real-world images. Expanding the dataset to include additional sources with varied object distributions, scene compositions, and cultural contexts would enhance the generalizability of our findings. %\sout{Additionally, our benchmark relies on GPT-4o for ground truth generation, which introduces potential biases inherent to this model. Exploring alternative methods, including human annotation or multi-model consensus approaches, could mitigate these biases.}

% Third, our evaluation framework depends on keyword-based parsing to assess model responses. While effective for structured prompts, this approach may not fully capture subtleties in free-text responses, leading to potential misclassification of certain psychological behaviours. A more sophisticated evaluation mechanism, possibly integrating open-source LLMs for response interpretation, could improve the robustness of our analysis. Keep it comment out first.

%\sout{Finally, while our human survey provides valuable comparative insights, it is limited in scale and demographic diversity. The surveyed population consisted of undergraduate students, which may not be representative of all human cognitive patterns. Future work should consider expanding the participant pool across different age groups, professional backgrounds, and cultural contexts to obtain a more holistic understanding of human-model alignment in psychological biases.}

Despite these limitations, our findings highlight important trends in VLM behaviour and offer a foundation for future research in AI psychology and hallucination mitigation strategies.


