\section{Main Results}
Following \cite{Tang2024LeveragingGL}, we use Accuracy (Acc.), macro-average precision (P), macro-average recall (R), and macro-average F1 (F1) to report the performance of our model in Table \ref{tab:results}. Our main observations are follows:
(1) Our model AdS achieves new state-of-the-art (Acc: $\delta$ 0.94\% in MMSD and $\delta$ 0.38\% in MMSD2.0) across unimodal as well as multimodal baselines. Owing to its shared adapter learning that enables efficient knowledge transfer between modalities and dual interaction module that caters to diverse sarcasm cases, AdS is able to overcome the limitations of earlier models. (2) We outperform MoBA with fewer parameters due to our effective cross-modal adapter sharing and optimal adapter placement strategy.

\subsection{Impact of Adapter Placement}
From Table~\ref{tab:adapter_placement}, we observe that placing adapters in the upper layers (7–12) of both visual and textual encoders yields the best performance. This aligns with the transformer hierarchy, where lower layers capture modality-specific features, while upper layers encode abstract, task-relevant semantics crucial for cross-modal reasoning in sarcasm detection.

\subsection{Comparison with PEFT Methods}

From Table \ref{tab:ads_ablation}, we observe that among various PEFT methods, 
adapters, LoRA, and prompt tuning, our proposed adapter-state sharing model achieves the best performance for multimodal sarcasm detection. Prompt tuning operates only at the input level and cannot model deep cross-modal interactions. LoRA modifies attention weights in a low-rank fashion but is limited in controlling hierarchical feature flow. In contrast, our adapter-state sharing design enables layer-wise, modality-specific adaptation while facilitating targeted cross-modal guidance by allowing textual adapters to inform visual ones. This mechanism improves the model’s ability to align sarcasm-relevant cues between text and image, resulting in significantly better performance with minimal parameter overhead.


