\section{Analysis}
To evaluate the strength of different components of our model, we design a few ablation experiments. From Table \ref{tab:ablation}, we observe that the variant  the variant \textit{AdS w/o Adapters} (both visual and textual adapters removed) shows reduced performance, highlighting the benefit of using multimodal knowledge of CLIP through shared adapter learning. The variant \textit{AdS V->T State Sharing} (visual adapters guide textual adapters) variant further degrades results, indicating that text adapters provides important cues to guide image adapters to attend to sarcasm specific regions. We also notice that \textit{w/o State Sharing} variant exhibits a drop in performance as the adapters lack cross-modal information sharing in this case.

