\begin{abstract}
Ambient backscatter communication (AmBC) has become an integral part of ubiquitous Internet of Things (IoT) applications due to its energy-harvesting capabilities and ultra-low-power consumption. However, the open wireless environment exposes AmBC systems to various attacks, and existing authentication methods cannot be implemented between resource-constrained backscatter devices (BDs) due to their high computational demands.
To this end, this paper proposes PLCRA-BD, a novel physical layer challenge-response authentication scheme between BDs in AmBC that overcomes BDs’ limitations, supports high mobility, and performs robustly against impersonation and wireless attacks.
It constructs embedded keys as physical layer fingerprints for lightweight identification and designs a joint transceiver that integrates BDs' backscatter waveform with receiver functionality to mitigate interference from ambient RF signals by exploiting repeated patterns in OFDM symbols.
Based on this, a challenge-response authentication procedure is introduced to enable low-complexity fingerprint exchange between two paired BDs leveraging channel coherence, while securing the exchange process using a random number and unpredictable channel fading. Additionally, we optimize the authentication procedure for high-mobility scenarios, completing exchanges within the channel coherence time to minimize the impact of dynamic channel fluctuations. Security analysis confirms its resistance against impersonation, eavesdropping, replay, and counterfeiting attacks. Extensive simulations validate its effectiveness in resource-constrained BDs, demonstrating high authentication accuracy across diverse channel conditions, robustness against multiple wireless attacks, and superior efficiency compared to traditional authentication schemes.


% Ambient backscatter communication (AmBC) has become an integral part of ubiquitous Internet of Things (IoT) applications due to its energy-harvesting capabilities and ultra-low-power consumption. However, resource-constrained backscatter devices (BDs) face significant security challenges, as traditional encryption-based authentication is impractical and existing physical layer methods are too computationally demanding.
% To this end, this paper introduces a novel physical layer challenge-response authentication mechanism (PL-CRAM) tailored for AmBC systems. The proposed PL-CRAM leverages embedded fingerprints as low-density identity (ID) keys, which are stored in backscatter device (BD) memory as lightweight identifiers. By exploiting channel coherence, BDs utilize amplitude shift keying (ASK) to modulate these ID keys, facilitating the efficient transmission and extraction of shared identity keys. This approach facilitates low-complexity authentication by leveraging the inherent randomness of wireless channel fading to secure challenge-response signals. Additionally, a joint transceiver design combines backscatter waveform generation with receiver functionality. By utilizing the cyclic prefixes of orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) symbols, the transceiver effectively mitigates interference from ambient RF signals, ensuring accurate detection of backscattered signals. The PL-CRAM mechanism is further optimized for high-mobility scenarios, ensuring that authentication exchanges are completed within the channel coherence time, thereby minimizing the effects of channel state fluctuations caused by mobility. Security analysis confirms its resilience against impersonation, eavesdropping, replay, and counterfeiting attacks, achieved through dynamic random numbers, shared key secrecy, and unpredictable wireless fading. Extensive simulations validate its effectiveness in resource-constrained BDs, demonstrating high authentication accuracy across diverse channel conditions, robustness against multiple wireless attacks, and superior efficiency compared to other AmBC authentication schemes.
\end{abstract}

