\section{Crossing Patterns in \MBST{}}
\label{com-sec}

In this section, we prove that \MBST{} is quasi-plane for every 2-colored point set in general position (Section~\ref{quasi-section}), and then use this \revised{result} to determine the maximum number of crossings in \MBST{} for a set of $n$ bichromatic points in the plane (Section~\ref{crossings-section}).}

\subsection{Quasi-planarity}
\label{quasi-section}
Let $S$ be a set of red and blue points in the plane. To differentiate between the points we denote the red points by $r_1,r_2,\dots$ and the blue points by $b_1,b_2,\dots$. Let $T$ be a MinBST for $S$. For two distinct edges $e_1$ and $e_2$ of $T$ we denote the unique shortest path between $e_1$ and $e_2$ in $T$ by $\spath{e_1}{e_2}$. This path contains exactly one endpoint of $e_1$ and  one endpoint of $e_2$.    

