\subsection{Configuring ICL}
% Many studies~\cite{gao2020nlpicl,jiang2020nlpicl,su2022nlpicl,levy2022diverse,lu2021order, Qin2023InContextLW} have focused on exploring how different configurations of in-context examples (ICEs) impact the in-context learning (ICL) performance of large language models (LLMs). Several studies propose using various metrics for selecting In-Context Examples (ICEs). For instance, Liu et al.~\cite{liu2022makes} suggest a kNN-augmented In-Context Example Selection method to retrieve the most similar ICEs for a given query example. Concurrently, COFE~\cite{an2023context} introduces an approach that considers Similarity, Diversity, and Complexity simultaneously. Additionally, Zhang et al.~\cite{zhang2022active} approach the ICE selection problem as a reinforcement learning task, tackling it with the Q-learning method. Li et al.~\cite{li2023finding} propose the use of an informative score metric to evaluate the effectiveness of an ICE sequence. Differently, Kim et al.~\cite{kim2022self} suggest generating demonstrations directly from the LLM itself. In addition to exploring the selection of demonstrations in ICL, some research focuses on studying the order of ICEs. Lu et al.~\cite{lu2022fantastically} initially demonstrate that the issue of order sensitivity in ICL is prevalent and suggest employing both global and local entropy metrics to determine the optimal sequence of examples while Liu et al.~\cite{liu2022makes} adopt a straightforward approach by positioning the most similar samples closest to the query. Another line of research explores the selection of a subset from unlabeled data to serve as a support set, which can subsequently reduce the overhead associated with ICEs selection. These methods rely on constructing a similarity graph and progressively updating the node weights after each sample selection, such as Vote-k~\cite{hongjin2022selective} and IDEAL~\cite{zhang2023ideal}.

Extensive studies in LLMs ~\cite{gao2020nlpicl,jiang2020nlpicl,su2022nlpicl,levy2022diverse,lu2021order, Qin2023InContextLW} have demonstrated that ICL performance is critically influenced by configuration factors including demonstration selection strategies~\cite{liu2022makes, an2023context,zhang2022active}, label composition~\cite{lyu2022z}, and demonstration ordering~\cite{lu2022fantastically,liu2022makes}. Current optimization approaches can be categorized into two paradigms:
heuristic-based and learning-based.
Heuristic-based methods primarily leverage semantic metrics to curate demonstrations. Early work focuses on retrieving examples with maximal semantic similarity to the query~\cite{liu2022makes}, while subsequent extensions incorporate diversity-aware selection criteria that balance relevance with category coverage~\cite{levy2022diverse}. These strategies effectively enhance ICL stability through controlled demonstration ensembles.
Learning-based approaches adopt data-driven frameworks to optimize ICEs configurations. A prominent direction involves training dedicated example encoders on annotated data to capture semantic features critical for context-aware selection~\cite{wu2022self}. Alternative formulations frame the configuration process as sequential decision-making tasks, enabling end-to-end optimization of demonstration impacts.

Given that the work mentioned above focuses on the NLP area within a single modality, some studies~\cite{wylnips, xu2024lmmicl,luo2024lmmicl,li2024configure, peng2023icd, baldassini2024lmmicl} have begun to explore the issue of ICL configurations for LMMs. 
% For example, Yang et al.~\cite{} propose adopting different strategies for images and texts to fully leverage their synergistic effects. Li et al.~\cite{} configure various retrieval strategies for the Visual Question Answering (VQA) task to investigate improvements in model performance. Peng et al.~\cite{peng2023icd} suggest modeling the selection of in-context demonstrations as a language modeling problem and train a tiny transformer for this purpose.
However, most existing studies have only explored simple and intuitive demonstration configuration strategies, falling short of a comprehensive investigation into multimodal ICL. There is also a lack of explanations for why certain strategies are effective or not.
Meanwhile, single-modal optimization methods struggle with cross-modal heterogeneity in multimodal tasks. 
Mismatches in image-text feature spaces can cause semantic conflicts~\cite{radford2021learning}, and imbalances of modal parameters favoring the language module lead to ignored visual clues~\cite{li2024configure, lyu2022z}. Existing strategies fail to detect contradictions from cross-modal information mismatches that cause reasoning deviations. 



