To quantify the anchor token aggregation observed in attention maps, we developed the ACAR metric based on attention weights as detailed in Section~\ref{internal}. Figure~\ref{fig:ar_results} (a) shows the mean ACAR curve across layers, revealing that attention values on anchor tokens are significantly larger than those on other context tokens. This confirms the attention aggregation effect on anchor tokens. 
Despite the consistent higher attention values on anchor tokens compared to context tokens, ACAR reaches particularly high values in the middle and later layers. The anchor phenomenon is more prominent in deeper layers than in shallower ones, indicating that there are more contextual interactions in earlier layers, while certain anchor tokens become carriers of attention information in deeper layers, prioritized during the forward pass to relay information.
Meanwhile, IDE exhibits a more pronounced anchor aggregation phenomenon than OF. 

