\section{Conclusion}
% In this work, we present a comprehensive investigation of in-context learning (ICL) in Large Multimodal Models (LMMs) for image captioning. Externally, we systematically explore demonstration configuration strategies through three critical dimensions with six metrics: ICE shot selection, image retrieval, and caption assignment. Our analysis reveals six key findings about how these factors collectively influence model performance. Internally, we identify three characteristic attention patterns through layer-wise analysis and propose novel attention-based metrics to quantify LMM behaviors. Auxiliary experiments further demonstrate the feasibility of leveraging sparse attention patterns for model acceleration and compression. Additionally, we compare the two LMMs in terms of their architecture and pre-training corpus, revealing how pre-training strategies and encoder choices affect ICL capabilities.
% These empirical insights—spanning both external configuration optimization and internal mechanistic patterns—provide concrete guidelines for future LMM development.

In this paper, we have carried out a comprehensive study on multimodal in-context learning for Large Multimodal Models for image captioning. Through systematic external and internal investigations, we have explored the impact of In-Context Example configurations on model performance and analyzed the internal attention mechanisms of LMMs. 
Externally, we have utilized multiple metrics to evaluate model performance, revealing key insights into how different ICEs configurations affect LMMs. 
Internally, we have identified typical attention patterns and developed attention-based metrics to quantify model behaviors. 
Our work demonstrates that both external and internal analysis perspectives are crucial for understanding and optimizing multimodal ICL in LMMs. The findings and proposed metrics offer valuable guidance for future research and development in LMMs, potentially applicable to broader research areas beyond image captioning.


