We prove Property \ref{prop2} by induction on $n$. Assume that the property holds for $n$ and let $q\in Q$, $\hat s_1,\hat s_2,\hat l\in\Nat$, $\tau'\in \varphi(T)$, $\theta\in(\Delta\setminus\varphi(T))^*$, and $\zeta'\in C_\Sigma(X_1)$ such that $\hat s_1\geq \hat l$, $\hat s_2\geq \hat l$, and \[(q,(\hat s_1,\hat s_2,\hat l))\Rightarrow_\A^{\tau'\theta'}\zeta'[(q_1,(s_1,s_2,l))]\Rightarrow^{\tau_1'\theta_1\ldots\tau_n'\theta_n}_\A\zeta'\cdot\zeta[(q_2,(s_1',s_2',l'))]\,.\] By IH, $(q_1,s_1-l,s_2-l)\Rightarrow_M^{\varphi^{-1}(\tau_1')\ldots\varphi^{-1}(\tau_n')}(q_2,s_1'-l',s_2'-l')$. We proceed by case distinction on $\tau'$:

Let $\tau'=(q\to\gamma(q'(2,1,1)))$. Then $q'=q_1$, $\hat s_1=s_1-2$, $\hat s_2=s_2-1$, and $\hat l=l-1$. By construction, $\varphi^{-1}(\tau')=(q,\inc(1),q_1)\in T$. Then $(q_1,\hat s_1-\hat l,\hat s_2-\hat l)\Rightarrow_M^{\varphi^{-1}(\tau')\varphi^{-1}(\tau_1')\ldots\varphi^{-1}(\tau_n')}(q_2,s_1'-l',s_2'-l')$. The case 
$\tau'=(q\to\gamma(q'(1,2,1)))$ is analogous.

Let $\tau'=(q\to\sigma(<_1(0,1,1),q_1(0,1,1)))$. Then $q'=q_1$, $\hat s_1=s_1$, $\hat s_2=s_2-1$, and $\hat l=l-1$. Moreover, by Observation \ref{obs1} it follows that $\hat s_1>\hat l$ and, thus, $\hat s_1-\hat l>0$. By construction, $\varphi^{-1}(\tau')=(q,\dec(1),q_1)\in T$. Then we obtain $(q_1,\hat s_1-\hat l,\hat s_2-\hat l)\Rightarrow_M^{\varphi^{-1}(\tau')\varphi^{-1}(\tau_1')\ldots\varphi^{-1}(\tau_n')}(q_2,l_1'-l_2',s_1'-s_2')$. The case 
$\tau'=(q\to\sigma(<_1(0,0,0,1),q_1(0,0,0,1)))$ is analogous.

Let $\tau'=(q\to\sigma(=_1(0,0,0),q_1(0,0,0)))$. Then $q'=q_1$, $\hat s_1=s_1$, $\hat s_2=s_2$, and $\hat l=l$. Moreover, by Observation \ref{obs2} it follows that $\hat s_1=\hat l$. By construction, $\varphi^{-1}(\tau')=(q,0(1),q_1)\in T$. Then $(q_1,\hat s_1-\hat l,\hat s_2-l)\Rightarrow_M^{\varphi^{-1}(\tau')\varphi^{-1}(\tau_1')\ldots\varphi^{-1}(\tau_n')}(q_2,l_1'-l_2',s_1'-s_2')$. The case 
$\tau'=(q\to\sigma(=_2(0,0,0,0),q_1(0,0,0,0)))$ is analogous.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:semantics}}
\label{sec:proof-lem-semantics}
\begin{proof}
  The direction ``if'' can be shown by structural induction on \(d\).  Let \(d = s(d_1, \ldots, d_\ell)\) for some \(s \in S_q\) with \(\statepos(s) = w_1 \cdots w_\ell\).
Let \(q_1\), \ldots, \(q_\ell \in Q\) such that \(\gentree(s)(w_i) = (q_i, \bm{0})\) for each \(i \in [\ell]\).
It is easy to see that also
\((q, \bm 0) \Rightarrow^*_\A s\).
But then, using the induction hypothesis for \(d_1\), \ldots, \(d_n\), this computation can be extended to
\[s \Rightarrow_\A^* s \bigl[ \gentree(d_1) \bigr]_{w_1} \cdots \bigl[ \gentree(d_\ell) \bigr]_{w_\ell} = \gentree(d) = \xi \,\text{.}\]

For the direction ``only if'', observe that every computation \((q, \bm 0) \Rightarrow^*_\A \xi\) can be written such that it begins with
\[(q, \bm 0) = \zeta_0 \Rightarrow_\A^{\tau_1} \zeta_1 \Rightarrow_\A^{\tau_2} \cdots \Rightarrow_\A^{\tau_k} \zeta_k\]
for some \(k \in \Nat\), \(\zeta_1\), \ldots \(\zeta_k \in T_\Sigma(Q \times \Nat^m)\) and transitions \(\tau_1\), \ldots, \(\tau_k \in \Delta\),
where
\begin{itemize}
\item for each \(i \in [k]\), \(\tau_i\) is applied at position \(w_i\) in \(\zeta_{i-1}\),
\item for each \(i \in [k-1]\), \(\tau_i\) is of the form \(q \to \sigma(q_1(d_1), \ldots, q_n(d_n))\) such that \(d_j \ne \reset\) for one \(j \in [n]\), and \(w_{i+1} = w_i j\),
\item and \(\tau_k\) is of the form \(q \to \sigma(q_1(d_1), \ldots, q_n(d_n))\) such that \(d_j = \reset\) for all \(j \in [n]\).
\end{itemize}
Intuitively, the computation starts with tracing downwards the path where no reset operation takes place.

Then the computation above continues with a subcomputation \((q_i, \bm 0) \Rightarrow_\A^* \xi_i\) for all occurrences \((q_i, \bm 0)\), \(i \in [\ell]\) for some \(\ell \in \Nat\), of a tuple from \(Q \times \{\bm 0\}\) in \(\zeta_n\).  Assume that for these subcomputations it is already proven that there are \(d_i \in D_{q_i}\) with \(\gentree(d_i) = \xi_i\).  Then we construct a spine computation \(s\) of the form
\[(\hat q, \bm 0) = \zeta_0' \Rightarrow_{\A'} \zeta_1' \Rightarrow_{\A'} \cdots \Rightarrow_{\A'} \zeta_k'\]
in \(\A'\) by replacing, for each \(i \in [k-1]\), the label \((q,c) \in Q \times \Nat^m\) at position \(w_i\) from above by \((\hat q, c)\).
Note that \(s(d_1, \ldots, d_\ell) \in D_q\).  Moreover, \(\gentree(s(d_1, \ldots, d_\ell)) = \xi\).
\end{proof}
