\subsection{Uncertainty Quantification}
\label{sec:uqmethod}
To quantify the geometric uncertainty of fabricated metamaterials, we use the resulting generative model to generate Monte Carlo samples of as-fabricated geometries given the target nominal design as the input condition, i.e., $\mathbf{x}_{\text{fab}}^{(i)} \sim p_\theta(\mathbf{x}_{\text{fab}} \mid \mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}})$.
% Following the model’s adaptation to real-world conditions, the trained conditional Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Model (DDPM) models the conditional distribution \( p(\mathbf{x}_{\text{fab}} \mid \mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}}) \), where \( \mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}}, \mathbf{x}_{\text{fab}} \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times m} \), enabling the quantification of geometric uncertainties in fabricated designs. 
% By sampling from the learned distribution, the model generates plausible fabricated geometries \( \mathbf{x}_{\text{fab}} \) conditioned on a given nominal geometry \( \mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}} \). 
% Specifically, to generate $N$ samples of $\mathbf{x}_{\text{fab}}$ given a \( \mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}} \), we first sample $N$ instances of Gaussian noise with the same dimensionality as $\mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}}$. We then concatenate each noise sample with \( \mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}} \) and iteratively feed these combined inputs through the trained U-Net, progressing from the initial to the final timestep. 
% This process produces conditioned fabricated geometries that capture manufacturing uncertainties, reflecting complex variations between nominal and fabricated designs as informed by both synthetic and real-world data. 

The quantified uncertainties facilitate robust design optimization (RDO), where the nominal design is optimized while considering the presence of manufacturing uncertainties. This optimization problem can be formulated as:
\[
\min_{\mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}}} \left[ -F \left( f(\mathbf{x}_{\text{fab}} \mid \mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}}) \right) \right], \mathbf{x}_{\text{fab}} \sim p_{\theta}(\mathbf{x}_{\text{fab}} \mid \mathbf{x}_{\text{nom}}),
\]
where \( f \) maps the geometry to material properties or performance metrics, and \( F \) is any statistics considered in RDO (e.g., lower confidence bound that considers the worst-case scenario).
% optimizing for desirable material properties, allowing for the development of designs that account for potential fabrication deviations.


