\subsection{Real World Experiment}
\label{sec:realworldexperiment}

% The real-world experiment evaluates GUST's performance in generating metamaterial geometries using data from actual additively manufactured components, assessing its capability of capturing realistic manufacturing uncertainties. For validation, two additional, distinct nominal designs were employed to assess the models' generalization to unseen data. Qualitative comparisons in Fig.~\ref{fig: RealGen} illustrate GUST's superior ability to generate high-fidelity geometries that closely match ground truth, accurately capturing eroded void shapes, while baseline models produce artifacts or fail to reflect real-world variations. Quantitative evaluation through Kernel Density Estimates (KDEs) in Figure~\ref{fig: RealKDE1} demonstrates GUST's precise alignment with the elastic property distributions of real-world data across the validated designs. The use of a limited but targeted training set (15 designs with 64 samples each) and validation on two separate designs ensures a practical assessment of GUST's robustness. These results highlight the effectiveness of GUST's self-supervised pretraining and transfer learning strategy, enabling it to adapt to complex manufacturing uncertainties and outperform baselines in both fidelity and generalization to new designs in real-world settings.


