\section{Experiments}

In this section, we present experimental evaluations of multi-agent LLM systems using the Hidden Profile paradigm.
We begin by demonstrating that GPT-4.1 agents, in the three instantiated Hidden Profile tasks described in Section \ref{sec:task}, fail to match the accuracy of single agents in the Complete Profile condition (Section \ref{sec:exp1}).
These experiments also reveal information-sharing biases similar to those found in human groups, along with context-dependent behavioral nuances, such as heightened sensitivity to social desirability cues.

We then extend our evaluation to the \textit{HiddenProfile} benchmark,\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/YuxuanLi1225/HiddenProfile}} which includes six additional tasks adapted from the social psychology literature (Section~\ref{sec:exp2}).
Across all tasks, we find that LLMs consistently underperform relative to their single-agent counterparts with complete profiles, exhibiting similar collective reasoning failures.

Finally, we use the paradigm to explore possible mitigation for these failures by varying prompt tone along a cooperation–contradiction spectrum (Section \ref{sec:exp3}). 
We show that while cooperative agents suffer from over-coordination and groupthink in collective settings, contradictory agents foster greater perspective diversity and improve individual accuracy—but at the cost of group consensus.

