\subsection{Matrix Multiplication Using Graphene}
Graphene\cite{hagedorn2023graphene} is an intermediate representation for specifying data layout and data movement using the shape algebra of CuTe~\cite{CuTE}, a part of NVIDIA's CUTLASS library.  Graphene improves upon the interface for Triton by: (1) supporting more general data layouts of strided rectangular regions, as specified using a shape algebra; and, (2) generating the complex index expressions automatically through a mapping from the shape algebra.  A performance engineer writes a template in the Graphene IR, which is instantiated by the Graphene compiler. 

\enlargethispage{\baselineskip}

Figure~\ref{fig:lego_graphene_motivation} shows an example Graphene specification (\texttt{spec}).  Almost the entire spec is focused on describing the layout of both computation (green boxes) and data (blue boxes), and their composition (purple boxes) in a hierarchical fashion -- threads and blocks for the computation layout, and tiles for data layout in global memory, shared memory, and registers.  The \texttt{\#} character on the left hand side refers to thread and block descriptions, while the \texttt{\%} character precedes data and composition layouts.

An advantage of Graphene's
approach is the explicit description of layouts, using the same algebra for both computation and data layouts. 
The complex indexing expressions are derived automatically from these specifications. A Graphene user has fine-grained control of the resulting mapping than with Triton, but must provide significantly more detail regarding data movement and composition of threads and data.  

