\subsection{LEGO Improvements}
In this paper, we show how LEGO improves upon the interfaces for both Triton and Graphene.  Like Graphene, it derives index expressions from layout specifications, freeing the programmer from providing these low-level details. %with a more intuitive layout.
%In LEGO, matrix multiplication is represented as a composition of layouts, enabling the automatic generation of a Triton kernel, as shown in the table in Figure~\ref{code:CUDATranspose} (right). All complex access patterns in optimized matrix multiplication can be described through the composition of these three fundamental layouts. This approach differs from prior work in CuTe/Graphene \cite{Graphene}, incorporating the iteration space, data, and computation layouts. 
As compared to Graphene, LEGO eliminates explicit stride specifications in the layout definition (see Section~\ref{subsec:comparison-graphene}) and extends support to any
%runtime-dependent layouts, such as look-up tables (LUTs), thereby encompassing any 
bijective mapping from multidimensional coordinates to contiguous linear space, an aspect not supported by previous work. %Layouts are composed to form complex structures similar to those expressed in Graphene.
Moreover, LEGO is a building block for host compilers or templates.  Therefore, it can be used on computations beyond tensor code generation or specific GPUs, and this integration is demonstrated with the MLIR compiler, CUDA code, and the Triton compiler.


\section{LEGO Specification}
\label{sec:lego-spec}

Discussion is organized as follows: section~\ref{subsec:math-intuition}
presents in an intuitive fashion how the LEGO pieces are composed, 
section~\ref{subsec:lego-blocks} shows the LEGO grammar and uses it
to define the semantics of a LEGO ensemble from the semantics of 
individual pieces, and section~\ref{subsec:comparison-graphene} 
compares the expression of Graphene and LEGO layouts.

\subsection{Mathematical Intuition}
\label{subsec:math-intuition}

The LEGO framework elevates data layout to a first-class design
consideration by which the user can define a logical view of the
index space together with reordering transformations, which can
be (de)composed hierarchically and chained horizontally.

We start by recalling the well known {\em canonical bijections}, 
denoted $\mathcal{B}$ and $\mathcal{B}^{-1}$, that connect a 
multi-dimensional index to its corresponding flat index of the
physical representation. 
%memory 
%
In figure~\ref{fig:math-eqs}, \cref{2Dflat} shows the 2-D case, which
connects an array of type $\tau[n_1][n_2]$ to its flat correspondent
$\tau[n_1\cdot n_2]$, and \cref{qDflat} applies it $q$ times
to derive the $q$-D case. 
The canonical bijections are the glue that binds the
LEGO blocks horizontally and vertically; of note, they
%by definition, 
do not change the order in which elements are laid out in memory.

\enlargethispage{\baselineskip}

In our approach, the user may define some logical view, 
denoted $GroupBy$, of the flat index space 
\text{$0\ldots N$-$1$} as $n'_1\times\ldots\times n'_{d'}$.
%
The left side of figure~\ref{fig:math-eg} illustrates the case of 
$d'=2$ and $N = n'_1 \cdot n'_2 = 6 \cdot 4$, where the elements 
represent the flat index space.

The logical view can be reinterpreted in a hierarchy of some
$q$ levels of tiles, each one of the same dimensionality $d$:
\vspace{-2ex}\\
\[
(n^1_1\times\ldots\times n^1_d)\times\ldots\times(n^q_1\times\ldots\times n^q_d)
\]%\vspace{-2ex}\\
\noindent This step, formalized in \cref{eq:step2-1} by means
of a canonical bijection, is essentially a reshape operation that 
does not change yet the physical layout. 
%
The middle part of figure~\ref{fig:math-eg} uses $(d,q) = (2,2)$ to reshape the original flat index space as a $4$-D array $(n^1_1 \times n^1_2) \times (n^2_1\times n^2_2) = (2\times 2) \times (3\times 2)$. (Laying down its elements in increasing order of inner dimensions still results in the original $[0,1,\ldots,22,23]$.)
%demonstrates the
%case of $(d,q)=(2,2)$ resulting in a $4$-D array of indices 
%$(n^1_1 \times n^1_2) \times (n^2_1\times n^2_2) = (2\times 2) \times (3\times 2)$;
%one can observe that laying down the $4$-D index space in increasing order of inner dimensions results in the original flat space $[0,1,\ldots,22,23]$.

