Our implementation for generating Triton and CUDA code utilizes this approach in which the user supplies code containing placeholders while defining the layouts separately. The indexing code is then generated by the Python and C printers provided by SymPy. To enhance productivity in the Triton path, we have introduced specialized slicing syntax analogous to NumPy's slice notation~\cite{numpy}. Specifically, when a user employs a colon (\texttt{:}) to denote the entire dimension—specified through $TiledBy$—the system generates a corresponding \texttt{tl.arange}, whose bounds are derived from the layout specifications. Furthermore, Triton mandates that the upper and lower bounds of this range be known at the time of compilation. 

Additionally, we examined whether pre-expanding index expression terms before applying Sympy’s simplification routines (including our range simplification) would yield better performance compared to simplifying the expression in its original form. The idea was that expansion might expose additional opportunities for optimization. However, in the NW benchmark, not expanding the expression beforehand resulted in better performance by minimizing the total number of operations. To accommodate scenarios where pre-expansion might be advantageous, we developed a cost model that counts the operations in the generated expression and selects the variant with the lowest operation count. 
