%\textcolor{red}{In response to the limitations observed in previous research, our work introduces the Network Simulator-centric Compositional Testing (NSCT) methodology, leveraging the capabilities Model-Based Testing and Event-Driven Network Simulator to verify internet protocol implementations.} 

%\textcolor{red}{Unlike the \textit{INET} suite, which focuses mainly on model-based simulation within \textit{OMNeT++}, our approach directly tests real-world protocol implementations against dynamic and time-sensitive network scenarios. This bridges the gap between theoretical models and practical implementations and enhances the applicability of tests to real-world scenarios.}

%\textcolor{red}{An additional enhancement is NSCT capacity to dynamically manipulate and test network conditions, thereby enabling a more thorough investigation of protocols under diverse operational stresses. This includes handling complex timing properties, critical for protocols such as \texttt{QUIC}, where timing inaccuracies can significantly affect both functionality and performance. By integrating the timing control within the simulation environment, NSCT provides a more nuanced and effective approach to protocol validation, addressing both the deterministic and time-variant aspects of network behavior.}

%\textcolor{red}{However, despite these advancements, the NSCT methodology is not without its limitations. The integration of Ivy and Shadow, while powerful, introduces complexity into the setup and configuration process, potentially increasing the initial learning curve and setup time for new users.} 

%\textcolor{red}{Additionally, the reliance on detailed model specifications and high-fidelity simulation environments can result in increased computational demands. This may limit the scalability of our approach when dealing with extremely large-scale network simulations or protocols with a vast number of nodes.}

%\textcolor{red}{Moreover, while NSCT enables the reproducibility of network protocol testing, it still depends on the accuracy of the underlying models and simulation tools. Any inaccuracies in the initial model or errors in the simulation environment setup could lead to misleading results, which necessitates rigorous validation and verification of the tools themselves.}

%\textcolor{red}{Finally, while NSCT extends the determinism and reproducibility of network simulations, it may not fully capture the random behaviors and non-deterministic nature of real-world network environments. Future work should focus on integrating stochastic elements more comprehensively into the simulation environment to better mimic the unpredictability of real-world networks.}



