\section{Discussion and future work}\label{sec:futur}
\textcolor{blue}{Despite the effectiveness of the NSCT methodology, as it is a form of Model-Based Testing, NSCT's efficacy hinges on the accuracy of the underlying models used to represent network protocols.}

\textcolor{blue}{Moreover, its implementation is subject to certain constraints. Network simulators, such as Shadow, form the backbone of NSCT testing procedures. These simulators operate by intercepting and simulating system calls of the implementations under test. However, this interception mechanism may lead to limitations in the set of testable implementations. On the one hand, a simulator must implement all the syscalls present in the implementation under test to run it properly. On the other hand, busy loops (an anti-pattern not very used) introduce a lack of precision in time, as they do not wait through syscalls.}

\textcolor{blue}{It's worth noting that currently, the topology part of the testing process with NSCT necessitates scenario-based simulation, wherein the behavior of the network within the simulated environment is predetermined. This could be fixed by using a network simulator that supports dynamic topology.}

\textcolor{blue}{Indeed, within the current implementation of NSCT, it would be easy to use a different network simulator. It would only imply to adapt some automation scripts. In the same way, Ivy could be replaced by any tool implementing the NCT methodology, but it would require translation of Ivy models.}


Further improvement of the time module \textcolor{blue}{would} allow for a more thorough verification of different types of properties. Closer integration between Ivy's generation process and the time module could make generating events at specific time points easier. This adaptation would enhance the tool's precision and overall usefulness. 

Expanding the scope of the testing to include different congestion mechanisms in various protocols would provide more detailed insight into the details related to implementation and effectiveness. 

Applying the methodology and tools discussed here to a broader range and scale of \texttt{QUIC} implementations would better validate and improve reliability and security.% \textcolor{red}{i.e doing empirical experiments}.

%Another exciting avenue to explore is the synergy between formal models of network attacks and Shadow's capabilities. This could involve studying protocols such as MP\texttt{QUIC} \cite{DeConinck2017} or complex routing protocols to identify and mitigate potential vulnerabilities, thus strengthening the reliability of protocol specifications. 
Another promising area of research lies in the examination of the synergy between formal attacks models in network and Shadow's capabilities, especially considering the ongoing development of protocols like \texttt{MPQUIC} \cite{DeConinck2017}. Currently, \texttt{MPQUIC} is still in its draft phase, grappling with significant security considerations between two main solutions. Given Shadow's unique ability to create custom network topologies, our methodology stands to offer substantial assistance. It enables the modeling of both solutions under consideration for \texttt{MPQUIC}, providing a comprehensive framework to assess their security implications and vulnerabilities.
