\section{Design of Computational Model}

We built our computational model using advantage actor-critic~\citep{mnih2016asynchronous}, a deep reinforcement learning algorithm. Within the algorithm, we formalize the overall reward signal $r$ as a combination of the intrinsic, social reward $r_i$ and extrinsic, environmental reward $r_e$:

\begin{gather}
   r = r_e + r_i \, ,\\
   \label{eqn:reward_function}
   r_i = - \alpha \cdot \textrm{max}(\bar{c} - c_{\textrm{self}}, 0) - \beta \cdot \textrm{max}(c_{\textrm{self}} - \bar{c}, 0) \, ,
\end{gather}

\noindent where $c_{\textrm{self}}$ is one's own contribution level (i.e., the amount of pollution cleaned from the river), $\bar{c}$ is an estimated or observed average of the group's contribution levels, and $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are scalar parameters.

The intrinsic motivation function was parameterized with $\alpha \sim \mathcal{U}(2.4, 3.0)$ and $\beta \sim \mathcal{U}(0.16, 0.20)$ (see Figure \ref{fig:agent_pseudoreward}). It therefore primarily represents an aversion to falling behind the group mean \citep{hardy2006nice}, as well as secondarily reflecting an aversion to being taken advantage of by free-riding others \citep{rockenbach2006efficient}.

A paired \textit{t}-test indicates that agents received significantly less intrinsic reward than extrinsic reward across all episodes of the anonymous condition, $t(839) = 67.8$, $p < 0.0001$ and all episodes of the identifiable condition, $t(839) = 103.6$, $p < 0.0001$ (Figure \ref{fig:agent_reward_comparison}).

