\begin{abstract}
\textcolor{black}{The evolution of 5G wireless technology has revolutionized connectivity, enabling a diverse range of applications. Among these are critical use cases such as real-time teleoperation, which demands ultra-reliable low-latency communications (URLLC) to ensure precise and uninterrupted control, and enhanced mobile broadband (eMBB) services, which cater to data-intensive applications requiring high throughput and bandwidth. In our scenario, there are two queues—one for eMBB users and one for URLLC users. In teleoperation tasks, control commands are received in the URLLC queue, where communication delays occur. The dynamic index (DI) controls the service rate, affecting the telerobotic (URLLC) queue. A separate queue models eMBB data traffic. Both queues are managed through network slicing and application delay constraints, leading to a unified Lagrangian-based Lyapunov optimization for efficient resource allocation. We propose a DRL-based hierarchical optimization framework that consists of two levels. At the first level, network optimization dynamically allocates resources for eMBB and URLLC users using a Lagrangian functional and an actor-critic network to balance competing objectives. At the second level, control optimization fine-tunes the best gains for robots, ensuring stability and responsiveness in network conditions. This hierarchical approach enhances both communication and control processes, ensuring efficient resource utilization and optimized performance across the network.}

% The development of 5G wireless technology has enabled the emergence of critical applications such as real-time teleoperation, which requires ultra-reliable low-latency communications (URLLC), and enhanced mobile broadband (eMBB) applications that demand high bandwidth. The coexistence of these task-driven teleop applications in a shared 5G network requires an efficient and dynamic allocation of bandwidth while maintaining the dual criteria of quality-of-service (QoS) requirements and overall system stability. In this paper, we consider a scenario where telerobotic users and eMBB users share the same 5G network resources. In this scenario, the reference commands for telerobotic task execution are transmitted to the lower-level robots with a network delay. The robust optimal controller, based on the Razumikhin criteria, generates control gains for the robots in response to this delay. The robot's task execution performance is assessed through a dynamic index (DI), which impacts the service rate and, ultimately, the telerobotic (URLLC) queue. In addition to this, we introduced a separate queue model the eMBB data traffic. We integrate these two virtual queues with network slicing and application delay constraints, formulating a unified Lagrangian-based Lyapunov optimization problem for the upper-level network. We propose a DRL-based solution that leverages this Lagrangian functional and utilizes an actor-critic network to balance competing optimization objectives. The proposed DRL-based hierarchical optimization framework dynamically adjusts resource allocation to meet varying task requirements, thereby enhancing communication and control processes across the network. In a shared 5G network, some telerobotic users require both ultra-reliable low-latency communications (URLLC) and enhanced mobile broadband (eMBB) services, while other eMBB users demand high bandwidth. This coexistence poses challenges in resource allocation, requiring dynamic strategies to meet stringent quality-of-service (QoS) requirements and maintain system stability. In this paper, we explore a scenario where these users share 5G resources, addressing the unique demands of dual-service telerobotic users and optimizing overall resource management

\end{abstract}

