\textbf{Zero-shot evaluation and Prompt selection}

\begin{itemize}
    \item LID: Alias answers – all three models sometimes returned synonyms (e.g., Persian-Farsi, Fulfulde-Fula). We used GPT-4.1 with the prompt, "You are an expert in language identification. Select the languages that are exactly the same as the target language. Make sure they are exactly the same. Answer with only the list of languages that are the same as the target. Return None if they are not the same.
    Target language: {target_language}
    Predicted languages: {Identified_languages}"
    We then cross-checked these responses manually to make sure they were correct, and We mapped these back to the canonical tags before scoring.
    \item ASR / S2TT:Introductory phrases – prepended lines like 'The audio says:', 'The English translation is:', 'The transcript is:'. We remove such headers with a simple regex before scoring.For the ASR, we used the regex `re.compile(r'((?:The|This) (?:[A-Za-z]+ )?(?:transcript|transcription)(?:ion)?(?: of the [A-Za-z]+(?: audio| speech))?(?: is(?: in [A-Za-z]+)?)?:|(?:The|This) [A-Za-z]+ speech is transcribed as follows:) ')` to remove the preamble. Gemini has no preamble. GPT-4o had few, but Qwen 2 had a lot. For the S2TT, we used `re.compile(r'((?:The|In) .+?(?:\bsays\b|\bstates\b|translates to|\bis\b|translates as|of the .+? (?:speech|sentence) \bis\b|of the .+? \bis\b|speech translates to):) ')`. In this, Gemini had no preamble, but Qwen 2 did. This indicates how Qwen 2 is not good at following instructions, followed by GPT-4o.

    Moreover, some refusals, but very rare (“Sorry, but I cannot provide a transcript…”).
    \item Topic Classification: Label drift – Qwen 2 often produced categories outside the allowed set. Gemini and GPT-4o stayed on the list nearly always.
\end{itemize}

In audio evaluation, GPT-4o follows formatting instructions best followed by Gemini. Qwen 2 needs the most post-processing.

