\paragraph{Speech-based results}
Table \ref{tab:overall_by_region_speech} shows the performance differences between models \qwen, \gpta, and \gemini in multilingual audio tasks. In LID, \gpta and \gemini demonstrate competitive performance, with \gpta showing advantages in Americas/Oceania ($98.1$ vs $96.7$) and Western/Northern/Southern Europe ($86.1$ vs. $84.5$), while \gemini performs better in other regions. For Topic Classification, \gemini and \gpta achieve similar high performance levels, substantially outperforming \qwen in all regions. In ASR, \gemini demonstrates superior performance with consistently lower Word Error Rates than \gpta and \qwen in all regions. Speech translation (\textbf{xx→eng}), evaluated on \qwen and \gemini, demonstrates Gemini's clear superiority with consistently higher ChrF++ scores in all regions. In general, \gemini achieves the best average performance, followed by \gpta, while \qwen consistently performs poorly in all the tasks and regions evaluated.

Figure \ref{fig:gemini_S2TT_eng_xx_translation_by_region} shows the translation performance of \gemini in different regions, measured using CHRF++ scores for bidirectional translation between English and other languages. European regions achieve the highest scores, with Eastern Europe reaching 62.2 for translations into English (\textbf{xx→eng}) and Western/Northern/Southern Europe scoring 59.5 for translations from English (\textbf{eng→xx}). Asian regions show moderate performance with notable variation, while Africa (37.5/39.0) and Oceania (27.2/45.8) show lower scores. The model exhibits directional asymmetry in several regions, particularly Asia (E), where \textbf{xx→eng} translation (52.9) significantly outperforms \textbf{eng→xx} translation (30.5). Overall, translation into English (53.5) slightly outperforms translation from English (49.0), though this pattern varies considerably by region, suggesting that both geographic-linguistic factors and translation direction influence Gemini-2-flash's multilingual capabilities.

\paragraph{Text-based results}
In Table~\ref{tab:overall_by_region_text} we report results of text-based evaluation using three models, broken down by geographic region. The metric used for MT tasks is ChrF++ and for all other tasks it is accuracy. For MT we report both directions of translation from other languages to English, and English to other languages (in that order). We also present the average for a region across the four classification tasks. 

We observe that for almost all tasks and regions, the best performance comes from proprietary models- GPT-4o or Gemini-2.0 Flash as opposed to the open-source Gemma 3 27B model. Gemini-2.0 Flash has the best macro and micro averages on all tasks except NLI, where GPT-4o performs the best. Looking at the average on classification tasks, Gemini-2.0 Flash performs best in Africa, Americas/Oceania, Asia (S), and Asia (SE) and GPT-4o performs best in Asia (W, C), Asia (E), Europe (W, N, S), and Europe (E). The average on classification tasks ranges from 74\% for Africa to 90.8\% for Europe (W,N,S). 

We see a large gap in performance across regions. On LID, Gemini-2.0 Flash has 80\% accuracy in Africa compared to near perfect accuracy in Asia (W, C) or Asia (E) denoting almost a 20 point difference. On MT, there's a 20 point gap in \textbf{xx→eng} and almost a 25 point gap in \textbf{eng→xx} when looking at Africa and Asia (E). The average on classification tasks shows Africa and America/Oceania lagging 13-17 points behind Asia (E), Europe (W, N, S), and Europe (E). 
