\paragraph{Topic Classification}
%Jesujoba (LID, SIB-200)
The results show that model \gemini{} outperforms the other models on this task, with model \gemma{} 
 and \qwen{} achieving the lowest performance on text and speech respectively. Additionally, \gemini{}'s classification accuracy on the text modality is noticeably higher than its performance on the speech modality. This indicates the model's stronger ability to understand and perform on text-based inputs. Region-wise, for the text modality, \gemini{} outperforms the other models in most regions except for the Americas/Oceania and Europe, where \gpt{} performs better. For speech, \gpt{} only shows better performance in East Asian languages. In addition, for \gemini{}, Africa is the lowest-performing region in the text modality, while the Americas/Oceania region has the lowest performance in the speech modality.

\paragraph{Reading Comprehension}
In Reading Comprehension, text-based evaluations (Table \ref{tab:overall_by_region_text}) indicate that \gemini{} 
 delivers the strongest performance, achieving macro and micro average accuracies of 83.9\% and 83.4\%, respectively, followed closely by \gpt{} with 82.8\% (macro) and 82.3\% (micro). The open-weight model, \gemma{}, scored lower at 70.2\% (macro) and 65.4\% (micro). Text-based reading comprehension accuracy was notably high in regions such as Asia (E) (87.7\% for \gemini{} and Europe (W, N, S) (90.3\% for \gemini{}. Transitioning to speech-based reading comprehension (Table \ref{tab:overall_by_region_speech}), \gemini{} again led with average macro and micro accuracies of 33.6\% and 33.3\%. \qwen{} achieved 21.7\% (macro) and 20.2\% (micro) in this modality. A marked performance decrease is evident when comparing modalities for \gemini{}, whose macro average accuracy in reading comprehension fell from 83.9\% in text to 33.6\% in speech. This substantial gap highlights the increased difficulty of comprehension tasks within the speech domain relative to text. Moreover, this gap is particularly pronounced for Africa and the Americas, underscoring again that multimodal support for languages of those regions must still catch up.

\paragraph{Translation tasks}
The analysis of MT capabilities reveals a notable performance disparity when comparing text-based MT with speech-to-text translation (STT), as shown in Table II and Table III. For text-based MT, \texttt{Gemini-2.0 Flash} generally outperforms \gpt{}. In the xx$\rightarrow$en direction, \gemini{} achieves a macro average ChrF++ score of 58.0, compared to 54.8 for \gpt{}. For the en$\rightarrow$xx direction, \gemini{} scores 42.9, while \gpt{} scores 41.0 on macro average.

In STT (xx$\rightarrow$en), which inherently combines ASR and MT, performance is lower than text-only MT. \gemini{} maintains a strong lead with a macro average ChrF++ score of 51.2. \gpt{} achieves a macro average of 33.1 in this modalitywe . When directly comparing xx$\rightarrow$eng performance, the transition from text input to speech input results in a performance decrease for both models. For \texttt{Gemini-2.0 Flash}, the macro average ChrF++ score drops by 6.8 points (from 58.0 in text MT to 51.2 in speech ST). \gpt{} experiences a more substantial decrease of 21.7 ChrF++ points (from 54.8 in text MT to 33.1 in speech ST). This indicates that while both models find speech translation more challenging than text translation, \gemini{} demonstrates greater robustness in maintaining its translation quality when processing spoken language input compared to \gpt{}. Regionally, the performance drop can be particularly pronounced; for instance, in the Americas/Oceania region for xx$\rightarrow$en tasks, \gemini{}'s score fell from 52.6 (text) to 27.2 (speech), and \gpt{}'s score dropped from 49.0 (text) to 11.9 (speech). Conversely, for \gemini{}, the degradation was minimal for some regions like Asia (S) (59.6 text vs. 58.3 speech) and Europe (E) (63.9 text vs. 62.2 speech), suggesting variability in resilience across different language groups or regional audio characteristics. The overall larger gap observed for \gpt{} highlights the compounded difficulty of speech processing and translation for this model compared to text-only translation.
