\textbf{Zero-shot evaluation}

When evaluating model output, for language identification (LID), automatic speech recognition (ASR), speech translation (S2TT), or topic classification, it is common to see extraneous text that was not explicitly requested by the prompt.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{LID}: Alias answers – all three models sometimes returned synonyms: For example: 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Persian \textrightarrow Farsi
        \item Fulfulde \textrightarrow Fula
        \item Punjabi \textrightarrow Punjabi
        \item Filipino \textrightarrow Tagalog
        \item Nyanja \textrightarrow Chichewa, Chewa
    \end{itemize}
    We used GPT-4.1 with the prompt: "You are an expert in language identification. Select the languages that are exactly the same as the target language. Make sure they are exactly the same. Answer with only the list of languages that are the same as the target. Return None if they are not the same.\newline
    Target language: \{target\_language\}\newline
    Predicted languages: \{Identified\_languages\}"

    We then cross-checked these responses to make sure they were correct and mapped them back to the canonical tags before scoring.
    \item \textbf{ASR / S2TT}: The models often insert preamble phrases like
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The audio says:
        \item The transcript is:
        \item The transcript of the Luganda audio is:
        \item The English translation is:
        \item The translation of the audio is:
        \item The English translation of the Burmese speech is:
    \end{itemize}
    We remove such headers with a simple regex before scoring. For the ASR, we used the following regex to remove the preamble. Gemini has no preamble.
    \begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, basicstyle=\ttfamily\small, breaklines=true]
re.compile(r'((?:The|This) (?:[A-Za-z]+ )?(?:transcript|transcription)(?:ion)?(?: of the [A-Za-z]+(?: audio| speech))?(?: is(?: in [A-Za-z]+)?)?:|(?:The|This) [A-Za-z]+ speech is transcribed as follows:) ')
    \end{lstlisting}  

    \gpta{} had few, but \qwen{} had a lot. For S2TT, we used the following regex to remove the preambles.
    \begin{lstlisting}[language=Python, basicstyle=\ttfamily\small, breaklines=true]
re.compile(r'((?:The|In) .+?(?:\bsays\b|\bstates\b|translates to|\bis\b|translates as|of the .+? (?:speech|sentence) \bis\b|of the .+? \bis\b|speech translates to):) ')
 \end{lstlisting} 
 In this, \gemini{} had no preamble, but \qwen{} did. This indicates that \qwen{} is not good at following instructions, followed by \gpta{}.

    \item \textbf{Topic Classification}: Label drift – \qwen{} often produced categories outside of the allowed set (eg Security, History, News). \gemini{} and \gpta{} remained on the list nearly always.
\end{itemize}

In conclusion, \gpta{} follows the formatting instructions best followed by \gemini{}. \qwen{} needs the most post-processing.

\end{comment}   

